  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 225 PM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nOn Monday, the ridge flattens somewhat as a shortwave trough aloft \nand its associated cold front enter our counties from the north, \ncooling off temperatures rather significantly across our northern \ncounties and there may be enough available moisture for isolated \nlate day thunderstorms over the southern Laramie Range. \n\nThen on Tuesday, expect a relief from the summertime heat in the \nwake of a passing Canadian origin cold front, sending maximum \ntemperatures back into the mid 70s to mid 80s. Moisture looks scant, \nperhaps enough for isolated to widely scattered late day \nthunderstorms near the southern mountains. \n\nFor your Wednesday...Brisk west northwest flow aloft continues and \nwith progged 700 mb temperatures near 18C, we anticipate maximum \ntemperatures to rise into the 80s. Models indicate a dry punch at \nlow and mid levels, effectively cutting off any convection chances \ndue to warm temperatures aloft producing plenty of CIN, Convective \nInhibition.\n\nThen on Thursday...The heat returns in the warm sector that our \ncounties will be in to the warm side of the cold front, with maximum \ntemperatures well into the 90s at most locations.  With scant low \nand mid level moisture, no showers or thunderstorms are expected.\n\nFor your Friday, the end of the traditional workweek...the heat \ncontinues with highs in the 90s. Not totally buying the GFS progged \nCanadian origin cold front sweeping across our counties and \nproviding heat relief, though your National Weather Service forecast \nteam will monitor future model trends to see if the cooler push will \ncome to fruition. Continued dry with limited atmospheric fuel, \nmoisture availability.",
